WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.379
When we talk about a recession, we're usually
talking about the state of the American economy

00:00:06.379 --> 00:00:10.030
in total in some kind of broader sense and
we're not there yet.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:14.080
I mean today we, we do not appear to be in
a recession.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.240
There are concerning signs that we've talked
about, but it would not be accurate or at

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:25.560
least defensible broadly to say that the country
is in a recession right now.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.589
But there are parts of the country that are
in a recession and at this point that is really

00:00:30.589 --> 00:00:32.610
impossible to deny.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.510
And the recession that exists already in parts
of the country are very highly connected to

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:39.130
Donald Trump's trade war with China.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:43.800
The also are connected to things like automation,
uh, and some other things that are bigger

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:44.860
than Trump.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.399
But Donald Trump's trade policy is certainly
precipitating this.

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:51.530
So let's first start with what is the definition
of a recession?

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:58.160
A recession is a period of temporary economic
decline during which trade and industrial

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:06.630
activity are reduced, generally identified
by a fall in GDP in two successive quarters.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:13.430
You need only look at places like Wisconsin
and Pennsylvania and a number of other states,

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:20.140
specifically in the industries of agriculture
and manufacturing to realize that we have

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:25.940
a regionalized, agricultural and manufacturing
recession right now.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:26.940
It's happening right now.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.880
This is not maybe at some point in the future.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:29.880
Okay.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:36.450
You have farms and farmers themselves experiencing
drastically reduced trade and industrial activity

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:42.760
to borrow the terminology from the definition
of recession because of a fall in their production

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.180
as a result of the trade war.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:53.100
You then have the sequential effects of related
drops in industrial activity at companies

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.260
that make farm equipment.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:02.040
Companies like Kune and John Deere Kune recently
furloughed hundreds of employees for two weeks

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.460
because they have slowed down production as
a result of farmers being hit by the trade

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:08.460
war.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:12.250
The trade war by shrinking farm trade is shrinking.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:17.880
The economic activity of companies like Kune
and others, so they are forced to cut production.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:23.220
And when you zoom out a little bit and you
look at the index of manufacturing activity,

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.250
you see a contraction last month for the first
time in three years.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:32.190
When you look at manufacturing jobs, the number
of new manufacturing jobs has dropped from

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:38.890
170,000 added at this time last year to just
44,000 barely enough to keep up with population

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:39.890
growth.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.930
This is stagnation in 22 states.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:48.930
The number of people working in factories
has fallen in the first seven months of 2019

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.550
according to the economic innovation group.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.790
So are we in a recession right now as a country?

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.720
We cannot say that at this point in time.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:02.819
At some point soon, we may be able to say
it is agriculture and manufacturing and a

00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:06.260
recession in a lot of the country.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.260
Yes it is.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.540
It is a regionalized recession that's happening
right now and you can actually go further.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:17.270
There are places where the 2008 recession
never really ended.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:23.900
The economically ravaged places in the Midwest
and elsewhere that simply never fully recovered

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.090
and are now already getting hit again.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.640
Now, in fairness, you know, I, one of the
things that I try to be consistent about is

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.610
whether it's a democrat or a Republican in
the White House, I assign blame only to the

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.400
extent that it is warranted and when there
are things that are bigger than who's in the

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.050
Oval Office, I tell you part of what's going
on is bigger than Trump.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.190
It's bigger than Trump's policy.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.900
There was a, a something called creative destruction.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.540
This is a mechanism in the business cycle.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:55.420
This was, uh, the, the term creative destruction
I believe comes from marks, but it meant something

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:56.420
else.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:00.349
Joseph Schumpeter, an Austrian economists
know left winger by any means.

00:04:00.349 --> 00:04:07.330
Uh, sort of took the term and described it
as an inherent feature of capitalist economies,

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:13.160
which is the constant revolutionizing of the
structure of the economy by changing the dynamics

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.040
of it and there is some degree of that.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.830
The United States is experiencing that there's
a move away from manufacturing in general.

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:24.860
There's a move towards tech and information
and other sectors.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:30.320
Parts of what we're seeing right now in manufacturing
could be certainly influenced by creative

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.030
destruction by automation.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:39.870
That is not Donald Trump's unique fault either,
but the bottom falling out this quickly specifically

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:46.260
because of Trump policy at a faster rate than
it would otherwise and nothing is being done.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.670
Big picture other than throwing some subsidies
at some farmers that is Trump.

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:58.060
Trump's trade war according to an estimate
from Moody's has killed 300,000 to jobs in

00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:59.060
total.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:00.070
So this is not good.

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:05.310
This is of course, potentially the, the, the,
the early indicator of things to come, not

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:11.260
in a recession right now broadly, but we don't
have to hypothesize about the future because

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:15.570
we have a regionalized manufacturing and agricultural
recession happening right now.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:19.970
Go and look at the really good interviews
with farmers and manufacturing companies,

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:24.790
farm equipment, manufacturing companies that
media outlets like NPR have done.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.270
It's really good work.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:33.130
It's happening right now, and to pretend it
isn't is a disservice to so many workers that

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:36.180
are already being affected by what's taking
place.

